TLS certificate files in Kubernetes etcd

etcd uses mutual TLS (mTLS): both sides present a certificate and both verify the other. In a kubeadm cluster, everything lives under /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ (plus one file in /etc/kubernetes/pki/).

Certificate files in a TLS handshake

When your computer talks to a server, two questions come up:

- Am I really talking to the right server? (not an imposter)
- How do we keep our conversation secret?

Certificates answer question 1. Encryption answers question 2.

## An analogy: government ID
Think of a passport:

A passport proves who you are → this is a certificate
The government that issued it is trusted by everyone → this is the CA (Certificate Authority)
Your signature, which only you can produce → this is the private key

## A border officer doesn’t know you personally. They trust the government that issued your passport, and they check your signature matches. TLS works the same way.


1. Private key (.key): your secret
Like your personal signature or the key to your house
Never share it
Used to prove you are who you claim to be

2. Certificate (.crt): your public ID card
Contains: your name/hostname, your public key, expiry date, and the CA’s signature
Safe to share with anyone
It’s tied to your private key (they are a matching pair)

3. CA certificate (ca.crt): the trusted authority
The “government” whose signature people trust
Used to check whether someone else’s certificate is genuine
Also safe to share

(The CA also has its own ca.key which it uses to sign certificates. Keep that very safe.)

.key	    Your signature (only you can make it)
.crt	    Your ID card
ca.crt	    The list of IDs the government approves


)):A tiny story:((

You walk up to a bank counter (the server).

The bank shows you its ID card (server.crt).
You check: “Was this ID issued by someone I trust?” You compare it with your ca.crt.
If yes, you trust the bank and start talking.

The bank also has a signature (server.key) that it never shows anyone. It uses it to prove the ID card really belongs to it.

Who needs what

- Normal website (one-way):
Server has: .crt + .key
You have: ca.crt

- Kubernetes (two-way, both show ID):
Server has: .crt + .key + ca.crt
Client has: .crt + .key + ca.crt

((:: ::((
.key = secret. .crt = ID card. ca.crt = how I check other people’s ID cards.

----------------------------------------------------------------
1) client makes a request to server

2) Server sends server.crt
      ↓
3) Client checks server.crt using ca.crt   ← "is the ID genuine?"
      ↓
   *** Server uses server.key to sign something ***   ← "is this ID really yours?"
   Client verifies that signature using the public key inside server.crt
      ↓
4) Client sends client.crt
      ↓
5) Server checks client.crt using ca.crt
      ↓
   *** Client uses client.key to sign something ***
   Server verifies it using the public key inside client.crt


